Suspense boundaries let you coordinate which parts of your UI should always
“pop in” together at the same time, and which parts should progressively
reveal more content in a sequence of loading states. You can add, move, or
delete Suspense boundaries in any place in the tree without affecting the rest
of your app’s behavior.

Don’t put a Suspense boundary around every component. Suspense boundaries
should not be more granular than the loading sequence that you want the user
to experience. If you work with a designer, ask them where the loading states
should be placed—it’s likely that they’ve already included them in their
design wireframes.

